{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a significant figure in the Crusader states during the High Middle Ages, known for his leadership and contributions to the Crusader cause. Born in 1140, he was a member of the House of Poitiers, a noble family with ties to the French crown. His father, Raymond II, was the Count of Tripoli, and his mother, Elvira of Castile, was a powerful figure in her own right.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\nRaymond III's reign was marked by his efforts to maintain and expand the Crusader states in the Levant. He ascended to the throne in 1152 after his brother William's death, succeeding his father. His rule was characterized by a series of conflicts with neighboring Muslim powers, particularly the Zengid dynasty and Nur ad-Din. Raymond III actively participated in military campaigns, including the Siege of Damascus in 1148 and the defense of the Crusader states against Muslim invasions. His leadership during these times was crucial in maintaining the Crusader presence in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\nIn addition to his military activities, Raymond III was involved in diplomatic efforts to secure alliances and treaties with various Christian and Muslim powers. He worked to strengthen the Crusader states through strategic marriages, including his own marriage to Eleanor of Châtillon, which helped to solidify his position and expand his influence in the region. His marriage to Eleanor, daughter of Baldwin II, King of Jerusalem, was a significant political move that helped to ensure the loyalty and support of other Crusader leaders.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\nRaymond III's reign also saw the construction and development of numerous castles and fortifications in the Crusader states. These structures were vital for the defense of the region and served as strategic points for launching offensives against 